A||N||9||Monkeyman||Alternate p/mirror switch||||4||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||09-03-2001||03:11 PM||||For those of you who have manual mirrors and want to upgrade to p/mirrors but can't find a wiring harness or switch, here's an idea.  This was my idea, but Phil is the real genius as he was the one to figure out the wiring.<P>All you need is:  a pair of p/mirror heads, a joystick type p/mirror control (mid '80s-early '90s Ford Escort although there may be other Fords that use this), and some wiring supplies.  The Ford joystick p/mirror switch fits perfectly in the stock manual mirror housing (on the drivers door) and keeps the "stock" look.  All you have to do is run a ground and power wire from the new switch and run 3 wires to each mirror.  Hook everything together and VOILA!!  You now have p/mirrors.<P>Kudos to Phil R. who came up with the correct wiring.  This is really an easy mod.  The toughest part is running the wiring through the doors and underneath the dash. <P>Here are some pics of the installed switch.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Image008_3.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Image009_2.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Image010_1.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Image011_1.jpg"> <P>(Don't mind the dirt and dust.  I'm trying to finish up what I can for Fierorama.)<P>------------------<BR>'Consciousness:  That annoying time between naps.'<P>MM||||reg||4
Z||000001||p-did||09-03-2001||04:28 PM||||NICE!  I'd rather have my controls there instead of in the center.  Good job guys!<P>------------------<BR>Jay Olson<BR>(AKA "fropuff")<BR>'86 GT, smashed rear fascia, fastback clip, right tail lamp assembly||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierohoho||09-03-2001||04:54 PM||||Monkey man that is awesome.<BR>Is there any way you could post the wiring diagram?<BR>How much room did you have to run the wires from the switch? <P>I know on my manuals the control is under strain which has bent the door panel near the control, did you have this problem and if so, did the swap fix it?<P>Thanks, HOHO<P>------------------<BR>'84 SE 2.5l<BR>'85 Coupe 2.5l now getting a 3800<BR>'86 SE 2.5l<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Pennockpic.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Wolfhound||09-03-2001||05:21 PM||||MM that's sharp. Does the rotation arrow on the lever select which window it operates?||||reg||14
Z||000004||Monkeyman||09-03-2001||09:13 PM||||p-did--Thanx.  Just remember.  Phil did all the hard work.  I just came up with the idea.<P>fierohoho--I swapped over to p/mirrors a while ago so I don't remember any strain on the door panel.  The only thing I can think of is that the metal connector on the manual mirror control is very stiff (has to be to work) and this is nothing but a switch and wires making it very flexible.  Just keep the wiring out of the way of the window.<P>Wolfhound--Yup.  Twist it to the left operates the left mirror and vice versa.  I've never been a Ford fan, but they did come up with some slick ideas, eh?<P>Here's the wiring diagram for those that want to attempt this:<P>Ford switch<BR>-----------<BR>Black 12V neg (ground)<BR>Green/Yellow 12V+ (power)<BR>-----You can tap into any power wire at the fuse block.  Phil was smart enough to suggest using an inline fuse for extra safety.<P>White---Green + White (both wires)  Pass side<BR>Purple--Yellow  Pass side<BR>Green---Blue  Pass side<P>Yellow--Green + White (both wires)  Drivers side<BR>Red-----Blue  Drivers side<BR>Blue----Yellow  Drivers side<P>I hope this helps.  If you have any questions, feel free to email me.  I'll help if I can.  BTW--I found the switch at a boneyard about a year or so ago for around $5.  If you can't find one at a boneyard, Ford still sells them for around $40 but you can't get any part of the wiring harness without buying the entire thing which runs around $150.  I'll keep looking around here to see if any of the boneyards in my area have any extra.  If so, I'll buy up what I can.  <P>------------------<BR>'Consciousness:  That annoying time between naps.'<P>MM<p>[This message has been edited by Monkeyman (edited 09-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||rockcrawl||09-03-2001||10:15 PM||||There are GM vehicles that use a joystick like that with the twist to select R/L. Cadillac for example uses them but they are ugly chrome. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||cadaver||09-03-2001||11:49 PM||||Nice. I would go that route if I didnt already run the factory switch and get another door panel.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||09-04-2001||02:41 AM||||Tom--My car was originally a manual mirror car.  I swapped door panels and installed the p/mirror harness, switch, and mirror heads.  This was just too cool to pass up.  I ended up cutting the mounting hole in the p/mirror door panel (I hated to cut a hole in that since they're in such demand, but I have the bottoms of the door panels covered in custom stitched vinyl and wasn't about to pay another $100 to have the other, non p/mirror panel recovered).  This was an easy job once I got things figured out.<P>rockcrawl--Chrome is for luxury barges and big trucks.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I didn't know that other GM vehicles used a similar setup.  I wonder if there are any other GM models that have a joystick switch that would fit and still look good?  No idea about the wiring, though.<P>------------------<BR>'Consciousness:  That annoying time between naps.'<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000008||GOLDSE||09-04-2001||11:15 AM||||MonkeyMan, my wife's 97 Cavalier has that type of power mirror switch.  It mounts on the window "wedge" on a little panel.  Turns L or R and is black.  Might work for your application.||||reg||1
